Import `math` and `operator` for the calculator implementation; `math` is unused here.

Define `OPS` as the mapping from the four supported operator symbols to their corresponding arithmetic functions.

## tokenize
Convert `text` into a list containing floating-point numbers and operator symbols. Accumulate consecutive digits and decimal points into each number, flush it before an operator or other nonnumeric character, ignore whitespace, and raise `ValueError` for any other character. Convert completed numeric strings with `float`; malformed numeric forms can therefore also raise `ValueError`. Return an empty list for empty or whitespace-only input.

## evaluate
Evaluate a token list from left to right without operator precedence. Return `0.0` for an empty list; otherwise use the first token as the initial result and apply each subsequent operator to the following value through `OPS`. The function assumes an alternating number/operator/number structure; incomplete or extra tokens are not explicitly rejected, and unsupported operators raise `KeyError.

## calc
Tokenize the expression in `text` and pass the resulting tokens to `evaluate`, returning its numeric result or propagating any tokenization or evaluation error.

## test
Add unit tests for the calculator functions.

When executed as a script, import command-line arguments, join all arguments with spaces into one expression, evaluate it with `calc`, and print the result. With no arguments, evaluate an empty expression and print `0.0`; errors are not caught.
